From 7a0173b4088419aa932b31deb5711ace816038f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cedric Robert <cedric.robert@f2i-consulting.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:54:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Sized reads: Span view, ten sized virtual reads on
 AbstractHdfProxy, extent check in HdfProxy

Reading an array through AbstractHdfProxy takes a bare pointer, so a proxy
never knows how many values the caller expects: an HDF5 proxy cannot check
the dataset extent against the buffer, and an ETP proxy has to ask the
store for the array metadata before every read, one round trip out of two
on a remote store, only to learn a count the caller already had.

This first patch adds the sized form without changing anything existing:

- common/Span.h: COMMON_NS::Span<T>, a non-owning view on a buffer and its
  size, the C++17 stand-in for std::span<T> with the same constructors and
  accessors, so that it becomes an alias of std::span the day fesapi
  requires C++20 without touching the call sites.
- AbstractHdfProxy: ten readArrayNdOf...Values(datasetName, Span<T>)
  virtuals, one per value type, whose default implementation forwards to
  the pointer form, so every existing proxy (SWIG directors included) keeps
  working; and readArrayNd<T>(datasetName, Span<T>), a template dispatching
  on T, the one entry point for generic code.
- HdfProxy (HDF5): overrides the ten sized forms and refuses, with a
  std::range_error, a dataset holding more values than the buffer.
- Unit tests: the Span view, and the sized reads on the existing HdfProxy
  test dataset (exact buffer, generic dispatch, conversion, too small a
  buffer refused, pointer form untouched).

The pointer forms stay the primary API for now; the next patches give the
AbstractObject helpers a sized form and move the representations to it,
after which the pointer forms can be deprecated.
